response.text:
{
  "packet_id": "56f6844980d1a9c6",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 separates the objective, deciding whether investment is justified, from the solutions. It lists do-nothing and route-to-market alternatives, and flags that reach to individual buyers is unknown and must be discovered, which is a useful constraint. It falls short of 3 because the alternatives are muddled. Alternative 1 offers 'industrial hearing protection' as a new consumer use case, although Hellberg already serves industry. Alternative 2 mentions 'fashion-oriented hearing aids', a different product category. A1 also misses that hlb-junior shows Hellberg already sells use-case-specific consumer products (children, concerts, lawn mowing)."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9"],
      "reason": "T1 (A9) says who changes behaviour (leisure consumers who see protectors as lifestyle accessories) and why (they buy several pairs for different contexts). It also names a value-capture route (premium pricing, direct-to-consumer). It is not a full chain to value this manufacturer captures. It assumes a direct-to-consumer channel even though A1 says reach to individual buyers is unknown. It never explains why Hellberg rather than a competitor would capture the premium."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1, fashion-led consumers buying direct (A9), and T2, compliance-led buyers through industrial distributors (A10), are genuine rivals with different customers and channels. Score is held at 2 because T2 largely describes Hellberg's existing safety business rather than a differentiated consumer proposition beyond it. So the set does not offer two real answers to the case question."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1's predictions in A9 are observable: purchase intent at least 15% higher at a 20% premium, and repeat purchase of at least 30% within 12 weeks. These thresholds are assumptions, not observations. Failing the purchase-intent prediction would hurt T1. A low 12-week repeat rate for a durable product would not clearly refute it, so the predictions are not fully diagnostic. T2's prediction compares a compliant protector with a 'generic non-compliant model', which is close to trivially true."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14", "A15", "A16", "A17", "A18", "A19", "A20", "A21", "A22", "A23", "A24", "A25", "A26"],
      "reason": "Every source is correctly labelled a company assertion and rated neutral. Each has a 'does not show' note, and no assertion is treated as demand evidence. The notes are generic, though. They do not mention the date (2017 figures in A17) or place. Only A19 addresses fit to the customer group (children versus adults). The assessments also miss that competitor segmentation in sdn-shop and sdn-x2 (A18, A25) bears on the competitive landscape that A1 said matters."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A13"],
      "reason": "There is only one challenge search, against T1 (A13). Its query ('consumer surveys fashion hearing protection demand') looks for demand rather than disconfirmation. There is no challenge search against T2. The results were reported honestly as neutral and no contradiction was forced, but the search effort is minimal."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A27"],
      "reason": "The test in A27 recruits a fashion-conscious group and a hazardous-work group and expects each to prefer matching messaging. Both predictions can hold at once, so the outcome is largely built in by the sampling and cannot show which proposition Hellberg should pursue. It reports 'percentage point' thresholds on a 7-point scale, which do not match. The decision rule is truncated at 'If neither'. It measures stated intent rather than real behaviour or reach, the gap A1 flagged. Cost and duration (EUR 12,000, 10 weeks) are proposed assumptions within budget."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A29"],
      "reason": "A29 correctly notes that SYN-D is simulated and concerns warehouse packing, not wearers or buyers. Keeping T1 unchanged is therefore a justified, evidence-linked retention. It does not explicitly state the (null) implication for the recommendation, so it does not reach 3."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A28", "A30"],
      "reason": "A30 favours T1 even though both beliefs are rated 'uncertain' and every source was neutral, so the preference has no evidence behind it. The reversal conditions contradict each other. A28 and A30 first say 'otherwise, maintain the current product line'. A30 then says 'no significant difference or a ≥15-point advantage for fashion messaging' leaves the recommendation unchanged and Hellberg 'could pursue' fashion. The timeline also conflicts: 12 weeks in A30 versus 10 weeks in A27. The value proposition relies on an asserted 'existing brand reputation' that no source shows applies to consumers."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "non_discriminating_test",
      "event_ids": ["A27"],
      "reason": "Segment-matched messaging preferences are expected under both theories at once, and the samples are pre-selected to produce them. The decision rule is truncated, so the test cannot separate T1 from T2 or identify which opportunity is worth investing in."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A28", "A30"],
      "reason": "T1 is favoured, and a fashion-led direct-to-consumer proposition is built on 'existing brand reputation', although every assessment was neutral and no source shows consumer demand or reach."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A28", "A30"],
      "reason": "The decision logic is internally inconsistent. The 'no difference' outcome leads to 'maintain current line' in one place and 'could pursue fashion' in another. A fashion advantage is listed under 'would change if' while also being said to leave the recommendation unchanged."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A1", "A10"],
      "reason": "The framing treats industrial or compliance hearing protection, Hellberg's existing core business, as a new differentiated use case. It also lists hearing aids as an adjacent hearing-protection segment."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "The episode is complete and the irrelevant simulated update was handled properly (A29). It is rejected because the proposed test does not discriminate between the theories (A27), the favoured theory has no evidential support (A28, A30), and the recommendation's reversal logic is self-contradictory (A30). These are critical reasoning errors, despite reasonable source labelling."
}

{
  "packet_id": "a8180a4199c64bfe",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 separates the objective (justified investment based on demand or a viable route to market) from candidate solutions. It lists real alternatives, including no expansion and a channel-only change (alt 4). It also flags a useful unknown: 'Current reach to individual buyers is unknown.' It does not reach 3. It never notices that Hellberg already sells consumer use-case products: hlb-junior names 'school- and hobby activities, concerts, lawn mowing or sporting events.' Instead, alt 3 describes the current business as 'B2B/industrial', which is an untested premise. Alt 1 also lists 'construction, or industrial safety' as consumer use cases, which blurs the consumer focus. The €20,000 / 12-week budget does not appear in the case packet and cannot be verified from it."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "The best theory, T1 (A9), says who changes behaviour (style-conscious consumers) and why (social signalling). It then names value capture (premium pricing through consumer channels). The chain is thin. It does not explain why Hellberg in particular would capture the premium, given its small scale (hlb-owner) and the specialist consumer competitors (iso-founded, uvx-consumer). T2 (A10) shifts buyers to employers and B2B channels and adds speculative regulator or insurance subsidies, though these are framed as assumptions."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 3,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 (lifestyle and status, consumer retail) and T2 (activity-specific performance and compliance, specialised and B2B channels) are genuine rivals. They imply different products, customers and channels. Caveat: T2 partly drifts away from the consumer question by including employers and contractors."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "Assumptions are explicit and predictions are observable, such as purchase intent '≥15% higher' or '≥20% higher' than a standard industrial model, and stated reasons for choice. The thresholds are proposed assumptions, not observations. Failing them would not clearly put either theory in trouble. Both theories compare against a generic industrial model rather than against each other, so the theories' own predictions do not cleanly separate them."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A15", "A16", "A17"],
      "reason": "Each assessment labels its source a company assertion and states a 'Does not show' limit, which is good practice. Three problems hold the score at 1.\n1. A17 clips hlb-junior to its first sentence, then claims the excerpt 'does not indicate whether the product is marketed for specific … activities.' The omitted second sentence names specific activities.\n2. A15 drops the SEK 66 m sales figure and the ~20 employees from hlb-owner. Both bear on feasibility, and the 2017 date is never noted.\n3. The competitor sources iso-founded and uvx-consumer, which bear directly on consumer-market competition, are retrieved repeatedly (A3–A8, A13) but never assessed.\nNo source is judged for date, place or fit with the customer group."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A13"],
      "reason": "There is a single search against T1 (A13). Its results (iso-founded, hlb-head, hlb-junior) are never assessed or reported as finding or not finding contradiction. No search is made against T2. This is a token gesture rather than a genuine search against the favoured theory."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A14"],
      "reason": "A14 directly compares a lifestyle version with a specialised version, so it can separate T1 from T2. Cost and timeline (€12,000, 10 weeks) and the 500-person sample are proposed assumptions. The design has several weaknesses:\n- It is labelled 'choice-based conjoint' but describes a randomised two-arm exposure with binary intent, which is internally inconsistent.\n- The thresholds are asymmetric, and the case where neither threshold is met is undefined. The decision rule is truncated ('high‑').\n- Stated intent from a panel is not demand.\n- It does not test either proposition against no expansion or a channel change (alts 3–4), nor the unknown buyer reach flagged in A1.\nIt does discriminate between the two theories but falls short of decision-relevant."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "not_reached",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": ["A18"],
      "reason": "Revision was declared applicable and an update was scheduled (scheduled_update true), but it was never delivered (update_delivered false). The episode stopped at A18."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "not_reached",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": ["A18"],
      "reason": "A18: 'The investigation stopped before a recommendation (repeated errors).' No recommendation was produced. This contributes zero to the all-case composite."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "incomplete",
      "event_ids": ["A18"],
      "reason": "The episode stopped before any recommendation, and the scheduled update was never delivered."
    },
    {
      "kind": "misleading_quote",
      "event_ids": ["A17"],
      "reason": "hlb-junior is clipped to 'specially designed for children.' The omitted sentence lists specific use cases ('concerts, lawn mowing or sporting events'). The assessment then asserts the excerpt does not indicate activity-specific marketing, which reverses what the full source shows."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "The framing treats Hellberg's current business as 'B2B/industrial' without support. The dossier shows consumer-oriented products (hlb-junior) and retail availability (hlb-retail), and these were never weighed."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "The episode is incomplete: it has no recommendation and the revision stage was not reached (A18). It also contains a misleading clipped quote (A17) and an unsupported framing premise (A1). Either the incompleteness or the clipped quote alone is enough to reject it."
}

{
  "packet_id": "86dfbce980048320",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 states an evidence-based objective and lists options. Alternatives 2 and 3 are effectively the same choice: keeping one professional range is the same as no expansion. The framing does not correct the premise that individual buyers are a new segment, although the dossier shows Neumann already reaches home-studio owners. It also misses constraints such as the post-pandemic contraction of the home-studio market."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A7"],
      "reason": "T1 in A7 says who changes behaviour (committed home-studio owners upgrading from starter gear) and why (they want pro-grade sound). The value-capture step is only asserted: premium entry-level microphones 'and interfaces' earn higher margins. Nothing addresses cannibalisation, channel or cost, so the full chain to captured value is not traced."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A7", "A8"],
      "reason": "T1 (sound quality and the upgrade path) and T2 (brand prestige and status) are genuine rival explanations of buyer motivation. Both imply the same product (premium entry-level), the same customer and the same channel, so they do not reach rung 3. No theory of weak or declining demand is developed, even though alternative 2 relies on one."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A7", "A8"],
      "reason": "The predictions are observable: survey shares, sales growth relative to the market, and a price premium. The thresholds (>60%, >50%, ≥20%) are arbitrary assumptions. Several predictions would not put the theory in serious trouble if they failed, for example 'sales higher where brand awareness is higher'. The two theories' predictions largely overlap."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10", "A2", "A6", "A11"],
      "reason": "A9 and A10 correctly label the two Academy sources as company assertions and say what they do not show. However, the only sources assessed are these two company claims, both marked neutral. Three verified observations were retrieved and never assessed: Futuresource on market re-stabilisation and reduced consumer spending, MusicRadar on the upgrade buyer, and the Sennheiser focus on Neumann. There is no discussion of date, place or fit to the customer group."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A13", "A14"],
      "reason": "A11 does search against T1 and retrieves neumann-futuresource-restabilise, which credibly bears on demand weakness. That result is never reported, weighed or reflected in later events. Not reporting what a challenge search returned falls short of a genuine, fairly reported challenge."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A12"],
      "reason": "Both theories predict that a majority will choose Neumann. The WTP thresholds are nested: any result ≥20% satisfies both T1 (≥15%) and T2, and the 'citing prestige' comment is not part of the decision rule. Both branches lead to the same action, so the test cannot separate T1 from T2. It is a stated-preference conjoint with undefined 'generic' comparators, and the €12,000/8-week cost and n=500 are assumptions. It does inform the go/no-go threshold, but it does not discriminate between the rival theories."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "not_applicable",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": [],
      "reason": "Revision is excluded from packet.applicable_dimensions, and no update was scheduled or delivered."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A14"],
      "reason": "A14 commits to a next action (run X1 within budget) and states conditions that would reverse it (<55% choice or <15% WTP leads to keeping one range). It acknowledges uncertainty. However, it favours T1 with no evidence distinguishing it from T2, since both are 'uncertain'. Its value proposition blends T1 and T2 ('while signaling status'). It ignores the contrary market-contraction evidence, so it only partly follows from the evidence."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "non_discriminating_test",
      "event_ids": ["A12"],
      "reason": "The T1 and T2 predictions overlap: both expect a majority choosing Neumann, and the WTP thresholds of ≥15% and ≥20% are nested. Both branches of the decision rule lead to the same action, so the test cannot separate the rivals."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A14"],
      "reason": "T1 is declared the favoured theory although both beliefs remain 'uncertain' and no assessed evidence favours T1 over T2. A14's value proposition also asserts a premium-margin, status-signalling product line that no source supports."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A7"],
      "reason": "Value capture through 'entry-level microphones and interfaces' at premium margins is asserted without any supporting source. No source in the dossier mentions Neumann interfaces or margins."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A2", "A3"],
      "reason": "Contrary evidence was retrieved and ignored. neumann-futuresource-restabilise (a verified observation of market contraction and reduced consumer spending) appeared in A2 to A5 and in the A11 challenge search, but it was never assessed or reported."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "The episode is complete, but the proposed test in A12 cannot separate T1 from T2. The favoured theory and value proposition (A13, A14) are unsupported by the assessed evidence, and the challenge search (A11) retrieved credible contrary evidence that was never assessed. These are critical reasoning errors, so the trajectory is rejected despite a reasonable next action and stated reversal conditions."
}

{
  "packet_id": "3e0ef7d87e1f1586",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 mostly restates the question as an objective and lists four obvious options. It does not separate the underlying goal from the proposed solution in a useful way. It also introduces a doubtful premise, calling Deere's model 'B2B', when its dealers sell to farmers. It sets the constraint 'public sources published before mid-1962' but never applies it to the sources."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9"],
      "reason": "For T1, A9 names who changes behaviour (small-garden households), why (a cheap, multi-chore tool versus manual work) and a value-capture route (existing dealers). The chain is thin. 'Avoiding new distribution costs' is asserted without support. The dealer evidence that bears on capture (deere-ctf-dealers) is never connected to the mechanism."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 and T2 share the same customer (homeowners), product (lawn and garden tractor), channel (existing dealers) and value capture. They differ only in the motive framing, 'practical/inexpensive' versus 'convenient/time-saving', so they are rewordings of one idea. Rivals such as licensing or no-entry, listed in A1, are never developed."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "The assumptions are stated, such as income, dealer willingness and brand trust. The predictions are weak. 'Consumer surveys from 1962 will show at least 30%' and 'at least 25%' use arbitrary thresholds about surveys not shown to exist. 'Dealers will order sufficient units within 12 weeks' is vague. None is framed so that its failure would clearly endanger the theory."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14", "A16", "A18", "A21", "A23", "A24", "A25"],
      "reason": "Source classes are relabelled throughout. Verified observations become 'company_assertion' (A12, A24) or 'independent_study' (A11, A14, A21, A25). A12 treats Deere's own later decision to enter the market as support for T2, which is neither demand evidence nor available in mid-1962. A14 and A16 rate deere-ctf-dealers as neutral, although it directly bears on the dealer-willingness assumption both theories share. Date and fit limits are never stated, for example the 1963 source in A18 and A23 and the 1869 source in A21 and A25. The 'does not show' notes are generic boilerplate."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A14", "A16"],
      "reason": "A13 searches against T1 and reports finding 'deere-ctf-rivals', a source not in the dossier. It never assesses that source or reports what it says. The results it did assess are marked neutral without saying that no contradiction was found. The challenge is nominal and its reporting is unreliable."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A26"],
      "reason": "A26 proposes an 'online survey' via a panel, which is anachronistic and infeasible in 1962. It is titled a 'Dealer Survey' but surveys homeowners. The dollar WTP thresholds ($1,000 and $1,500) do not match a 7-point WTP scale. Both arms presuppose the dealer channel, so the test compares message framings and cannot address whether to invest or which channel to use. The method and decision-rule text is truncated. Cost and sample are assumptions within budget, but the design does not separate meaningful rivals."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "not_applicable",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": [],
      "reason": "Revision is excluded from packet.applicable_dimensions, and no update was scheduled or delivered."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A27", "A28"],
      "reason": "A28 takes no investment position. It only recommends running the flawed survey while favouring T1 with both beliefs 'uncertain'. The uncertainty and reversal conditions are truncated, and the reversal conditions rest on the invented WTP thresholds. The recommendation does not answer what evidence would justify investment beyond this one test."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A12"],
      "reason": "Deere's own later decision to enter the consumer market (deere-green-venture) is treated as support for a homeowner-demand theory. A company decision is not evidence of demand, and the source falls outside the mid-1962 information constraint set in A1."
    },
    {
      "kind": "invented_observation",
      "event_ids": ["A13"],
      "reason": "The challenge search reports finding 'deere-ctf-rivals', which does not exist in the dossier."
    },
    {
      "kind": "non_discriminating_test",
      "event_ids": ["A26"],
      "reason": "Both arms assume purchase through existing dealers and differ only in message framing. The test cannot separate rivals about whether to invest, which customers to target or which channel to use. The WTP thresholds are inconsistent with the 7-point measure, and the decision rule is truncated."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A12", "A18", "A23", "A24"],
      "reason": "Reliance on hindsight. The 1963 production fact and the eventual market-entry decision are used in a mid-1962 analysis, contrary to the constraint in A1."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14", "A16", "A21", "A24", "A25"],
      "reason": "Source classes are systematically mislabelled. Verified observations are recast as company assertions or independent studies, which distorts the evidence weighting."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A14", "A16"],
      "reason": "deere-ctf-dealers is misread as neutral. It shows that Deere dealers were already selling rival lawn tractors, which bears directly on the dealer-channel assumption and on value capture."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A26"],
      "reason": "The proposed method is anachronistic: an online panel survey in 1962 is infeasible."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "The episode is completed but contains critical errors. These are an invented source in the challenge step (A13), a company decision and hindsight source treated as demand support (A12), and a non-discriminating, anachronistic test (A26). Source classes are systematically mislabelled, and the two theories are near-rewordings. The recommendation is to run a flawed test, and its reversal conditions are truncated."
}

{
  "packet_id": "4ae11bea47b27a98",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 separates an objective (evidence of demand, willingness to pay and a route to consumers) from the proposed solution, and lists five alternatives, including licensing and retail partnerships. It also rests on a false premise. A1 cites 'Jotun's current B2B focus' and offers 'continue focusing exclusively on B2B' as an option. The dossier contradicts this: jotun-pci-diy says Jotun serves 'the professional and DIY customer directly', jotun-pci-share puts Decorative Paints at 37% of sales, and jotun-pci-yachts says Marine already serves leisure yachts. The framing never recognises that Jotun already has consumer reach and leisure-boat customers. The €20,000 / 12-week budget is an assumption, not a case fact. Because the premise mis-states the starting point, the score is held below 2."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 (A9) says who changes behaviour (home boat owners), why (compliance and trust in marine expertise) and how value is captured (premium margins, brand extension). The full chain does not hold together. 'Leveraging its existing distribution network' is never linked to a channel that sells marine coatings to consumers; the Multicolor Centers in jotun-pci-diy serve decorative customers. The mechanism also assumes owners are compliance-motivated, which jotun-pmc-unknown (21% did not know their paint type) and jotun-pmc-illegal (14% used prohibited paints) put in question. This meets rung 2 but not 3."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 (compliance and trust) and T2 (convenience kit) give rival motivations for the same purchase. Both target the same customer (home boat owners), the same product base (Jotun marine coating) and the same channel. No theory covers a different use case, a different customer group (for example protective coatings for homeowners) or a null 'no material demand' rival, so rung 3 is not met."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "The predictions are observable: at least 15% purchase intent at a 20% premium for T1, and at least 20% intent for the kit at a 15% premium for T2. Their thresholds are arbitrary assumptions. The second prediction of each theory ('those who trust Jotun' or 'rate ease of use higher' report higher intent) is close to tautological and would hold under either theory, so failure would not clearly put either theory in trouble."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
